# SERVED ASSETS ARE BYTE-SENSITIVE. Their `?v=` pin is a content hash of exactly
# the bytes the Worker ships, and src/worker.js serves a pinned URL
# `immutable` for a year. So the deployed bytes must not depend on WHO ran the
# deploy — and without a rule here they do.
#
# core.autocrlf=true gives a Windows checkout a CRLF working tree. `wrangler
# deploy` uploads the working tree, so the same commit ships CRLF from one
# machine and LF from another. The content is identical either way; the HASH is
# not. Measured on 2026-08-16: `stamp-assets --check` reported 100 stale pins on
# a Windows checkout when every pin was correct for the bytes production was
# actually serving, and restamping there rewrote all 100 to CRLF digests. A
# deploy from an LF machine would have flipped all 100 straight back. That is a
# churn loop with no fixed point, and it makes the pin check — the thing standing
# between a changed file and a year of stale cache — untrustworthy in both
# directions: it cries stale when nothing changed, so the next person learns to
# restamp reflexively without reading what moved.
#
# Pinning these to LF makes the deployed bytes a property of the commit alone.
# LF rather than CRLF because that is what the repo blobs already hold and what
# production already serves for most of these files.
*.html   text eol=lf
*.js     text eol=lf
*.mjs    text eol=lf
*.css    text eol=lf
*.svg    text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.map    text eol=lf

# Vendored third-party libraries are byte-pinned.
#
# These files are loaded in the browser with a Subresource Integrity (SRI) hash. Git's
# core.autocrlf=true would rewrite LF -> CRLF on checkout, change the bytes, and break the
# SRI check — the browser would then refuse to execute the script. For DOMPurify that would
# silently disable the inbox composer (sanitize() throws when it is missing), so keep these
# byte-identical to what the SRI hash in the HTML was computed against.
#
# LAST ON PURPOSE: git applies the final matching rule, so this must sit below the
# `*.js text eol=lf` line above or vendored bytes would be normalised along with
# everything else and the SRI hashes would break — the exact failure this rule
# was written to prevent.
admin/js/vendor/** -text
